From: vyos-ls1046a-build CI <ci@local>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fman: port: O/H port re-enqueue infra, phase 1a-1g (ASK2 T-M6-8)

Consolidates the full "ASK2 OH-port re-enqueue infra, phase 1a" arc
(0175-0184, 10 patches, all dated 2026-09-01/02) into one patch:

  0175  bind Offline/Host-command (O/H) ports (phase 1a)
  0176  O/H bring-up: FMBM_O* register programming (phase 1b)
  0177  O/H bring-up hand-arm debugfs (phase 1c de-risk)
  0178  fix O/H port discoverability before first bring-up (phase 1c fix)
  0179  quiet spurious WARN_ON in O/H fifo-size verification (phase 1c polish)
  0180  O/H target-FQID bring-up + enable debugfs verb (phase 1d step 1)
  0181  fix fman_port_active_list double-insertion for O/H ports (phase 1c/1d fix)
  0182  O/H input FQ scheduling debugfs verb (phase 1e)
  0183  O/H frame injection + capture sink debugfs verbs (phase 1f)
  0184  O/H register + FQ-state readback debugfs verb (phase 1g)

All ten touch only fman_port.c (plus fman.{c,h}/fman_port.h in 0175/
0176), applied in strict sequence with nothing else interleaved.

0181 is not a routine review-slice and its full original narrative is
preserved verbatim below, since it documents a real board-found
incident whose root cause is two other patches in this same cluster:

Board-found 2026-09-01 while diagnosing an unrelated production VLAN
offload bug report on .185: a plain routed (non-VLAN) TCP flow through
a completely unrelated physical port (eth3, hw port 0x10) failed
hardware offload with "REPLACE rollback fe_flow_insert=-19" (ENODEV)
on every single attempt, forever. Traced to
fman_cc_tree_install()'s (dpaa_fman_caps.c) call to
fman_port_lookup_rx(fm, 0x10), which returned NULL for a port that has
been working since well before this session.

Root cause: 0177/0178 register an O/H port into fman_port_active_list
at PROBE time (fman_port_probe()) so a not-yet-brought-up O/H port is
discoverable by the "bringup" debugfs tool. But the ORIGINAL 0115
registration point, at the end of fman_port_init(), unconditionally
does its own list_add_tail() on the same port->node -- and this
session's Phase 1c/1d board testing did call "bringup 4" (dmesg:
"oh_test bringup 0x04 OK" at boot+140s), which drives
fman_port_config()+fman_port_init() for that same, already-probe-time-
registered O/H port object. That second list_add_tail() on an already-
linked struct list_head splices the node into the list a second time
without unlinking it from its first position, corrupting the shared
doubly-linked list for every port on the fman -- not just the O/H
port. fman_port_lookup_rx()'s list_for_each_entry walk over the
corrupted list then silently fails to reach unrelated RX ports
(eth3/0x10 in this case), and every caller (fman_cc_tree_install(),
i.e. every port's VLAN CC-tree AND plain ehash-vs-CC-tree dispatch)
gets -ENODEV. No crash, no WARN -- list.h's plain (non-DEBUG_LIST)
list_add_tail() has no double-add detection, so this was a silent,
board-wide production regression from a debug-only test tool call.

0175's probe-time registration comment claimed this was "safe" because
port->node is kzalloc'd/zeroed and list_add_tail() "only writes
through it, never reads it as an existing link first" -- true for the
FIRST call, but that reasoning never accounted for fman_port_init()
making a SECOND, unconditional call on the same node later. It also
can't be fixed with list_empty(&port->node) as the guard: kzalloc
leaves ->next/->prev NULL rather than self-pointing, since port->node
is never INIT_LIST_HEAD'd, so list_empty() is not well-defined for a
never-linked node here.

Fix: add struct fman_port::active_list_added (kzalloc-zeroed, so
false until set), checked and set under fman_port_active_lock at BOTH
call sites (fman_port_init()'s original 0115 site, and 0175/0178's
probe-time site). The node is now linked into fman_port_active_list at
most once regardless of call order -- probe-then-init (O/H ports) or
init-only (RX/TX ports, unaffected: active_list_added starts false, so
their existing single list_add_tail() still fires exactly as before).

Board-tested 2026-09-01: this exact double-registration state (from
the "bringup 4" call earlier in .185's current uptime) is why the live
VLAN offload investigation this session was giving inconsistent
results -- it was chasing a live-corrupted port registry, not a VLAN-
specific defect. A reboot is required to clear the already-corrupted
in-memory list on any board that already ran "bringup" before this
fix; this patch only prevents the corruption going forward.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate | Risk-Tier: A (adds one bool field + a
guard around two pre-existing list_add_tail() call sites; RX/TX ports
take the exact same single-add path as before, byte-identical
behavior for every port type except O/H, where it now prevents a
double-add instead of allowing one)

Verified against the real CI checkpoint commits in the persistent
kernel-git-cache (run 34563593304, checkpoints 96ca6c89c5b1 "applied:
0173..." to 3564a9b357a7 "applied: 0184..."), round-trip verified
byte-identical via `patch -p1`.

Touches: fman.c, fman.h, fman_port.{c,h}.

Signed-off-by: vyos-ls1046a-build CI <ci@local>
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman.c
@@ -2320,15 +2320,30 @@
 	if (fman->state->rev_info.major < 6)
 		set_port_order_restoration(fman->fpm_regs, port_id);
 
-	mac_id = hw_port_id_to_sw_port_id(fman->state->rev_info.major, port_id);
+	/*
+	 * ASK2 OH-port re-enqueue infra (dpaa1): O/H ports have no MAC, so
+	 * there is no per-MAC MTU to check or track here. Without this guard
+	 * hw_port_id_to_sw_port_id() -- which was only ever written for
+	 * RX/TX hw ids -- silently aliases every O/H hw id (0x02-0x07, all
+	 * below BASE_RX_PORTID) to mac_id 0, and the write below would
+	 * corrupt whichever real MAC happens to live at index 0 in
+	 * mac_mfl[]/port_mfl[]. Confirmed by reading hw_port_id_to_sw_port_id()
+	 * (fman.c) against BASE_RX_PORTID/BASE_TX_PORTID before writing this.
+	 */
+	if (port_params->port_type != FMAN_PORT_TYPE_OH) {
+		mac_id = hw_port_id_to_sw_port_id(fman->state->rev_info.major,
+						  port_id);
 
-	if (port_params->max_frame_length >= fman->state->mac_mfl[mac_id]) {
-		fman->state->port_mfl[mac_id] = port_params->max_frame_length;
-	} else {
-		dev_warn(fman->dev, "%s: Port (%d) max_frame_length is smaller than MAC (%d) current MTU\n",
-			 __func__, port_id, mac_id);
-		err = -EINVAL;
-		goto return_err;
+		if (port_params->max_frame_length >=
+		    fman->state->mac_mfl[mac_id]) {
+			fman->state->port_mfl[mac_id] =
+				port_params->max_frame_length;
+		} else {
+			dev_warn(fman->dev, "%s: Port (%d) max_frame_length is smaller than MAC (%d) current MTU\n",
+				 __func__, port_id, mac_id);
+			err = -EINVAL;
+			goto return_err;
+		}
 	}
 
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&fman->spinlock, flags);
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman.h b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman.h
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman.h
@@ -83,6 +83,15 @@
 enum fman_port_type {
 	FMAN_PORT_TYPE_TX = 0,	/* TX Port */
 	FMAN_PORT_TYPE_RX,	/* RX Port */
+	/*
+	 * Offline/Host-command (O/H) port -- RM 5.3.1 "FMan Hardware Ports
+	 * Types". No MAC; dequeues re-circulated frames from QMan, walks
+	 * Parser/KeyGen/CC via its own FMBM_O* BMI register block, then
+	 * re-enqueues. ASK2 OH-port re-enqueue infra (dpaa1); see
+	 * plans/ASK2-VLAN-REARCH.md and the board-patch series near
+	 * 0175-fman-port-oh-type.patch for the bring-up this unlocks.
+	 */
+	FMAN_PORT_TYPE_OH,
 };
 
 struct fman_rev_info {
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
@@ -14,6 +14,15 @@
 #include <linux/of_address.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/libfdt_env.h>
+#include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
+#include <linux/atomic.h>
+#include <linux/smp.h>
+#include <soc/fsl/qman.h>
+#include <linux/debugfs.h>
+#include <linux/err.h>
+#include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/string.h>
+#include <linux/uaccess.h>
 
 #include "fman.h"
 #include "fman_port.h"
@@ -273,10 +282,71 @@
 	u32 reserved0310[0x3a]; /* (0x310-0x3FF) */
 };
 
+/*
+ * BMI Offline/Host-command (O/H) port register map -- RM 5.5.4.7, register
+ * offsets confirmed against LS1046ADPAARM.pdf (each O/H port's block lives
+ * at FMan_base + 0x1000*n, n = hw O/H port id; ASK2 dpaa1 board uses n=0x04,
+ * live-confirmed unbound 2026-09-01, see plans/ASK2-VLAN-REARCH.md). Field
+ * order/offsets match the RM table exactly -- do not reorder.
+ */
+struct fman_port_oh_bmi_regs {
+	u32 fmbm_ocfg;		/* O/H Configuration */
+	u32 fmbm_ost;		/* O/H Status */
+	u32 fmbm_oda;		/* O/H DMA attributes */
+	u32 fmbm_oicp;		/* O/H Internal Context Parameters */
+	u32 fmbm_ofdne;		/* O/H Frame Dequeue Next Engine */
+	u32 fmbm_ofne;		/* O/H Frame Next Engine */
+	u32 fmbm_ofca;		/* O/H Frame Command Attributes */
+	u32 fmbm_ofpne;		/* O/H Frame Parser Next Engine */
+	u32 fmbm_opso;		/* O/H Parsing Start Offset */
+	u32 fmbm_opp;		/* O/H Policer Profile */
+	u32 fmbm_occb;		/* O/H Custom (Coarse) Classification Base */
+	u32 fmbm_oim;		/* O/H Internal Margins */
+	u32 fmbm_ofp;		/* O/H FIFO Parameters */
+	u32 fmbm_ofed;		/* O/H Frame End Data */
+	u32 reserved0038[0x2];	/* (0x038-0x03F) */
+	u32 fmbm_opri[PORT_PRS_RESULT_WORDS_NUM];
+	/* O/H Parse Result Initialization */
+	u32 fmbm_ofqid;		/* O/H Frame Queue ID */
+	u32 fmbm_oefqid;	/* O/H Error Frame Queue ID */
+	u32 fmbm_ofsdm;		/* O/H Frame Status Discard Mask */
+	u32 fmbm_ofsem;		/* O/H Frame Status Error Mask */
+	u32 fmbm_ofene;		/* O/H Frame Enqueue Next Engine */
+	u32 fmbm_orlmts;	/* O/H Rate Limiter Scale */
+	u32 fmbm_orlmt;		/* O/H Rate Limiter */
+	u32 fmbm_ocmne;		/* O/H Continuous Mode Next Engine */
+	u32 reserved0080[0x38]; /* (0x080-0x15F) */
+	u32 fmbm_ocgm[PORT_CG_MAP_NUM];	/* Observed Congestion Grp Map */
+	u32 reserved0180[0x20]; /* (0x180-0x1FF) */
+	u32 fmbm_ostc;		/* O/H Statistics Counters */
+	u32 fmbm_ofrc;		/* O/H Frame Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_ofdc;		/* O/H Frames Discard Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_ofledc;	/* O/H Frames Length Error Discard Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_ofufdc;	/* O/H Frames Unsupported Format Discard Cntr */
+	u32 fmbm_offc;		/* O/H Filter Frames Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_ofwdc;		/* O/H Frames WRED Discard Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_ofldec;	/* O/H Frames List DMA Error Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_obdc;		/* O/H Buffers Deallocate Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_oodc;		/* O/H Out of Buffers Discard Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_opec;		/* O/H Prepare to Enqueue Counter */
+	u32 reserved022c[0x15]; /* (0x22C-0x27F) */
+	u32 fmbm_opc;		/* O/H Performance Counters */
+	u32 fmbm_opcp;		/* O/H Performance Count Parameters */
+	u32 fmbm_occn;		/* O/H Cycle Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_otuc;		/* O/H Tasks Utilization Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_oduc;		/* O/H DMA Utilization Counter */
+	u32 fmbm_ofuc;		/* O/H FIFO Utilization Counter */
+	u32 reserved0298[0x1a]; /* (0x298-0x2FF) */
+	u32 fmbm_odcfg[0x3];	/* O/H Debug Configuration */
+	u32 fmbm_ogpr;		/* O/H General Purpose Register */
+	u32 reserved0310[0x3c]; /* (0x310-0x3FF) */
+};
+
 /* BMI port register map */
 union fman_port_bmi_regs {
 	struct fman_port_rx_bmi_regs rx;
 	struct fman_port_tx_bmi_regs tx;
+	struct fman_port_oh_bmi_regs oh;
 };
 
 /* QMI port register map */
@@ -425,6 +495,7 @@
 struct fman_port {
 	void *fm;
 	struct list_head node;		/* fman_port_active_list (0115) */
+	bool active_list_added;	/* guards list_add_tail() idempotency (0181) */
 	u32 ctrl_params_page;	/* FM_CTL params page MURAM off (0116) */
 	struct device *dev;
 	struct fman_rev_info rev_info;
@@ -608,6 +679,107 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * ASK2 OH-port re-enqueue infra (dpaa1): O/H equivalent of init_bmi_rx()/
+ * init_bmi_tx(), implementing RM 5.5.7.6 "Initialization Steps for Offline
+ * Ports" steps 1-8 (DMA attrs, internal-context params, dequeue/parser/
+ * frame next-engine, frame command attrs, FQID/EFQID, discard/error masks,
+ * enqueue next-engine). Steps 9-12 (rate limiter, congestion-group map,
+ * continuous-mode next-engine, stats counters) are all RM-documented as
+ * optional ("if desired"/"if necessary") and left at hardware reset (0) --
+ * not needed for the de-risk build. Step 13 (FMBM_OCFG[EN]) is
+ * DELIBERATELY NOT done here: this function leaves the port fully
+ * configured but disabled, matching this project's established
+ * dormant-until-armed pattern (fman_port_use_kg_hash() is likewise called
+ * separately from fman_port_init(), not from init_bmi_rx()). See
+ * fman_port_oh_enable() for the explicit arm step.
+ *
+ * FMBM_OFPNE (parser's own next engine) is set to plain BMI-enqueue here,
+ * the same safe default init_bmi_rx() gives fmbm_rfpne before
+ * fman_port_use_kg_hash() flips it to KeyGen -- the O/H equivalent flip
+ * (to KeyGen + CC-tree dispatch) is fman_port_set_oh_cc_base(), called
+ * only once a real CC tree exists, not from here.
+ */
+static int init_bmi_oh(struct fman_port *port)
+{
+	struct fman_port_oh_bmi_regs __iomem *regs = &port->bmi_regs->oh;
+	struct fman_port_cfg *cfg = port->cfg;
+	u32 tmp;
+
+	/* DMA attributes (step 1) */
+	tmp = (u32)cfg->dma_swap_data << BMI_DMA_ATTR_SWP_SHIFT;
+	tmp |= BMI_DMA_ATTR_WRITE_OPTIMIZE;
+	iowrite32be(tmp, &regs->fmbm_oda);
+
+	/* Internal context parameters (step 2) */
+	tmp = ((cfg->int_context.ext_buf_offset / PORT_IC_OFFSET_UNITS) &
+		BMI_IC_TO_EXT_MASK) << BMI_IC_TO_EXT_SHIFT;
+	tmp |= ((cfg->int_context.int_context_offset / PORT_IC_OFFSET_UNITS) &
+		BMI_IC_FROM_INT_MASK) << BMI_IC_FROM_INT_SHIFT;
+	tmp |= (cfg->int_context.size / PORT_IC_OFFSET_UNITS) &
+		BMI_IC_SIZE_MASK;
+	iowrite32be(tmp, &regs->fmbm_oicp);
+
+	/* Internal buffer offset margin (mirrors init_bmi_rx()'s fmbm_rim) */
+	tmp = ((cfg->int_buf_start_margin / PORT_IC_OFFSET_UNITS) &
+		BMI_INT_BUF_MARG_MASK) << BMI_INT_BUF_MARG_SHIFT;
+	iowrite32be(tmp, &regs->fmbm_oim);
+
+	/* Dequeue next engine (step 3): BMI kicks off via QMI dequeue */
+	iowrite32be(NIA_ENG_QMI_DEQ, &regs->fmbm_ofdne);
+
+	/* Frame next engine (step 4): once BMI has the fetched frame,
+	 * hand to the hard parser.
+	 */
+	iowrite32be(NIA_ENG_HWP, &regs->fmbm_ofne);
+
+	/* Frame command attributes (step 4). No MR bits: TX's own
+	 * BMI_CMD_TX_MR_DEF is 0 and O/H's confirmation semantics are
+	 * TX-like (see init_qmi()'s FMQM_OnEN comment) -- only order +
+	 * sync, matching what classification ordering needs.
+	 */
+	tmp = BMI_CMD_ATTR_ORDER;
+	tmp |= (u32)cfg->color << BMI_CMD_ATTR_COLOR_SHIFT;
+	tmp |= BMI_CMD_ATTR_SYNC;
+	iowrite32be(tmp, &regs->fmbm_ofca);
+
+	/* Parser next engine (step 5): plain enqueue until a CC tree is
+	 * armed via fman_port_set_oh_cc_base() -- see function comment.
+	 */
+	iowrite32be(NIA_ENG_BMI | NIA_BMI_AC_ENQ_FRAME, &regs->fmbm_ofpne);
+
+	/* Default/error queues (step 6) */
+	iowrite32be((cfg->dflt_fqid & DFLT_FQ_ID), &regs->fmbm_ofqid);
+	iowrite32be((cfg->err_fqid & DFLT_FQ_ID), &regs->fmbm_oefqid);
+
+	/* Discard/error masks (step 7) -- permissive (0) for the de-risk
+	 * build; O/H has no set_rx_dflt_cfg()-style discard_mask source.
+	 */
+	iowrite32be(cfg->discard_mask, &regs->fmbm_ofsdm);
+	iowrite32be(cfg->err_mask, &regs->fmbm_ofsem);
+
+	/* Enqueue next engine (step 8) */
+	iowrite32be(NIA_ENG_QMI_ENQ | NIA_ORDER_RESTOR, &regs->fmbm_ofene);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * ASK2 OH-port re-enqueue infra (dpaa1): explicit FMBM_OCFG[EN] arm/disarm
+ * (RM 5.5.7.6 step 13), kept separate from init_bmi_oh() -- see that
+ * function's comment. Returns -EINVAL on a NULL or non-O/H port.
+ */
+int fman_port_oh_enable(struct fman_port *port, bool enable)
+{
+	if (!port || port->port_type != FMAN_PORT_TYPE_OH)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	iowrite32be(enable ? BMI_PORT_CFG_EN : 0,
+		    &port->bmi_regs->oh.fmbm_ocfg);
+	return 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(fman_port_oh_enable);
+
 static int init_qmi(struct fman_port *port)
 {
 	struct fman_port_qmi_regs __iomem *regs = port->qmi_regs;
@@ -630,6 +802,27 @@
 		iowrite32be(NIA_ENG_BMI | NIA_BMI_AC_TX, &regs->fmqm_pndn);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * ASK2 OH-port re-enqueue infra (dpaa1): O/H QMI configuration.
+	 * Dequeue NIA (RM 5.5.7.6 step 3, "set QMI dequeue as the next
+	 * module in FMBM_OFDNE") is the BMI-side half, written by
+	 * init_bmi_oh(); this is the QMI-side half -- once QMI has
+	 * dequeued a frame it hands back to BMI to fetch it (mirrors the
+	 * RM's "fetch frame and execute" host-command action code, already
+	 * defined as NIA_BMI_AC_FETCH_ALL_FRAME 0x20c and otherwise unused
+	 * in this driver). Enqueue NIA uses the same release semantics as
+	 * the RX branch above (an O/H port's incoming buffer, like RX's,
+	 * needs releasing back to BM once processing completes) rather
+	 * than TX's wire-confirmation-oriented release -- least-certain
+	 * field in this bring-up; revisit if BMan pool behaviour under
+	 * sustained OH traffic suggests otherwise.
+	 */
+	if (port->port_type == FMAN_PORT_TYPE_OH) {
+		iowrite32be(NIA_ENG_BMI | NIA_BMI_AC_RELEASE, &regs->fmqm_pnen);
+		iowrite32be(NIA_ENG_BMI | NIA_BMI_AC_FETCH_ALL_FRAME,
+			    &regs->fmqm_pndn);
+	}
+
 	/* Dequeue Configuration register */
 	tmp = 0;
 	if (cfg->deq_high_priority)
@@ -731,6 +924,26 @@
 	case FMAN_PORT_TYPE_TX:
 		err = init_bmi_tx(port);
 		break;
+	case FMAN_PORT_TYPE_OH:
+		/*
+		 * ASK2 OH-port re-enqueue infra (dpaa1): deliberately no
+		 * init_hwp() call here, matching TX (RX is the only type
+		 * that calls it). Two independent reasons: (1) the RM's
+		 * O/H init steps (5.5.7.6) never mention per-port parser
+		 * HXS/PMDA/LCV setup at all -- only "set hard parser as
+		 * next module in FMBM_OFNE", which init_bmi_oh() already
+		 * does; (2) this project's own IPv6 dual-port-engage
+		 * investigation (2026-08-19/20) left open whether the hard
+		 * parser's PCAC stop/start is genuinely per-port or a
+		 * shared physical block across all ports -- init_hwp()
+		 * brackets its PMDA writes with a PCAC stop/start, and
+		 * calling that for a new O/H port while eth3/eth4 carry
+		 * live traffic is exactly the untested cross-port
+		 * interaction that investigation flagged as a real wedge
+		 * risk. Skip it; nothing in the O/H bring-up needs it.
+		 */
+		err = init_bmi_oh(port);
+		break;
 	default:
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
@@ -830,8 +1043,24 @@
 {
 	u32 min_fifo_size_required = 0, opt_fifo_size_for_b2b = 0;
 
-	/* TX Ports */
-	if (port->port_type == FMAN_PORT_TYPE_TX) {
+	/*
+	 * TX Ports. ASK2 OH-port re-enqueue infra (dpaa1) also takes this
+	 * branch, not RX's. Board-tested 2026-09-01 on .185: this upstream
+	 * function only ever handled TX/RX, so both sums stayed 0 for O/H,
+	 * tripping the WARN_ON below on every bringup call. Harmless --
+	 * fifo_bufs.num is never negative, so the dev_dbg verification a
+	 * few lines down was a silent no-op either way -- but noisy, and
+	 * worth fixing properly since O/H is architecturally closer to TX
+	 * here. Both are QMI-dequeue-driven: FMBM_OFDNE and FMBM_TFDNE both
+	 * use NIA_ENG_QMI_DEQ, see init_bmi_oh(). Every field this formula
+	 * reads is already populated generically for O/H: max_frame_length
+	 * and cfg->tx_fifo_deq_pipeline_depth by set_dflt_cfg() for every
+	 * port type, unconditionally; port_speed is simply 0 for O/H, which
+	 * only changes which b2b-margin constant applies below, not
+	 * correctness.
+	 */
+	if (port->port_type == FMAN_PORT_TYPE_TX ||
+	    port->port_type == FMAN_PORT_TYPE_OH) {
 		min_fifo_size_required = (u32)
 		    (roundup(port->max_frame_length,
 			     FMAN_BMI_FIFO_UNITS) + (3 * FMAN_BMI_FIFO_UNITS));
@@ -1208,6 +1437,16 @@
 		default:
 			return 0;
 		}
